The binary_galactica.glass backend is adapted from the gbonfly implementation
in wdm_test/wdm_paper_2/gbonfly, a JAX transcription of GLASS's fast-slow UCB
waveform and supporting routines. The comparison target is:
https://github.com/nsgs-analysis-center/glass
commit 079c6a3a6fcf380a7c107f39640fff7fc720d0f1.

GLASS portions are Copyright 2019 Tyson B. Littenberg and Neil J. Cornish,
licensed under Apache License 2.0, reproduced in LICENSES/Apache-2.0.txt.
The reference math routines are Copyright 2023 Tyson B. Littenberg and
Robert Rosati, under the same license.
This distribution modifies the JAX transcription's FFT normalization, adds
parameter adapters and validation, preserves documented waveform numerical
guards, and rejects invalid Fisher proposals as whole-vector self-proposals.

Version 0.3 adds a JAX implementation of GLASS's sparse-time spline TDI
response, using analytic paired differences and removable-limit guards.
It adds differentiable response interpolation and makes this path the default
waveform engine. The mainline uses reference-spacecraft phase removal and local
complex polynomials; a Hermite utility with local complex-minimum refinement
remains available for numerical comparisons. The original
fast-slow engine remains available explicitly as the Fourier backend.

The optional C reference regeneration tool contains extracted GLASS routines
and vendored Kiss FFT source. Their source provenance and individual license
notices are included under tools/glass_reference. Kiss FFT is not a runtime
dependency of the Python package.
The independent on-the-fly response reference and its provenance are in
tools/onfly_reference; it uses extracted GLASS spline-response routines with
analytic source and orbit evaluators in place of spline storage.
